 "VERDICT": "Three things are settled on the part of the ladder that has run and none of\nthem is a threshold.\n\n### Positional binding survives its own data fix\n\nAt 45M there are {{kpx_l45_count}} question shapes of the held-out sentence\nmode where the reader is exact on {{kpx_l45_key_n}} of {{kpx_l45_key_n}}\nkey-first items and on 0 of {{kpx_l45_value_n}} value-first ones:\n{{kpx_l45_shapes}}. Same shape, same group, same mode, same gold structures.\nOnly which of the two the sentence names first differs.\n\nThe seven shapes that are exact on no value-first item of that group each saw between {{keypos_seven_vf_min}} and {{keypos_seven_vf_max}}\nvalue-first training items, {{keypos_seven_share_min}} to\n{{keypos_seven_share_max}} of that shape's own draw, and between\n{{keypos_seven_per_mode_min}} and {{keypos_seven_per_mode_max}} of them inside\nevery one of the {{n_trained_modes}} sentence modes training contained. On\nthose modes the reader is {{kp_l45_train_value_first}} exact on value-first\nitems against {{kp_l45_train_key_first}} on key-first ones, over\n{{kp_l45_train_value_first_n}} and {{kp_l45_train_key_first_n}} items. It has\nthe order and it reads it. On a sentence mode it never saw, the same reader is\n{{kp_l45_mode_value_first}} on value first against\n{{kp_l45_mode_key_first}} on key first, and on seven shapes it is exact on\nzero value-first items out of 93.\n\nThe binding is in the text and it is not ambiguous there. The hand-written\nparser reads the same value-first half of that group\n{{parser_mode_value_first}}, and {{parser_mode_lookup_value_first}} on each of\nthe seven shapes taken separately.\n\nBalancing key position in the training draw cannot fix that, because the draw\nis already balanced and has been all along. What failed to transfer is the\ncombination of an axis value the network saw about forty thousand times per\nshape with an axis value it never saw at all, and no draw fixes that without\ngiving up the held-out test. This is a representational failure and not a\ndistributional one, and it is the first failure in this project that is known\nto survive the data fix that would be tried on it.\n\n### The transposed operand test is the other case and does have a data fix\n\nIts fine tuning draw carries one operand order on\n{{grid_train_canonical}} of {{grid_train_pages}} pages. Fine tuning on the\nbalanced draw instead takes the reading of the transposed page from\n{{tp_l45_transposed_all_exact}} to {{tpb_l45_transposed_all_exact}} and the\nuntransposed key order from {{tp_l45_transposed_all_key_order_canonical}} to\n{{tpb_l45_transposed_all_key_order_canonical}}. That one was a hole in a draw.\nIt costs the original version of the page,\n{{tp_l45_original_all_exact}} down to {{tpb_l45_original_all_exact}}, and the\ngroup it helps least is the held-out sentence mode, where the untransposed\norder still wins {{tpb_l45_transposed_mode_key_order_canonical}} against\n{{tpb_l45_transposed_mode_exact}} exact.\n\nUnder the original one-order draw the test does not move with scale at all. On\nthe `train` frame group the fine tuned reader is exact on the original page\n{{tp_l45_original_train_exact}} at 45M and {{tp_xl93_original_train_exact}} at\n93M, so it has the page shape, and on the transposed version of that same page\nit is exact {{tp_l45_transposed_train_exact}} and\n{{tp_xl93_transposed_train_exact}} while writing the untransposed key order\n{{tp_l45_transposed_train_key_order_canonical}} and\n{{tp_xl93_transposed_train_key_order_canonical}}. Doubling the parameters moved\nnone of it.\n\n### What would decide whether it is general\n\nThe reading above is that the reader has the value-first order on every\nsentence mode it trained on and loses it on one it did not. It does not say\nwhether that is about `relative_clause` in particular or about any unseen mode.\n`src/norm/ndata.py` withholds the mode through one constant, `HELD_MODE`, so\nthe check is that constant, a rebuild of `data/norm`, one rung at 30,000 steps\nand the same scoring, which is about three hours on this card. If a different\nwithheld mode also produces shapes exact on every key-first item and no\nvalue-first one, the failure is a property of the architecture and not of one\nsentence form.\n\n### What the ladder can and cannot say about parameters\n\nThe rungs share a token budget, not a budget per parameter. {{budget_steps}}\nsteps at a {{budget_cap}} source-token cap is {{budget_source_tokens}} source\ntokens and {{budget_target_tokens}} target tokens for every rung, which is\n{{tpp_l45_source}} source tokens per parameter at 45M against\n{{tpp_xl93_source}} at 93M. Counting only the tokens the loss falls on it is\n{{tpp_l45_target}} against {{tpp_xl93_target}}. Whichever count is used, the\n93M rung had half the tokens per parameter the 45M rung had.\n\nAt that budget, and at the 45M rung's own rate, the 93M rung fits its own\ntraining file worse than the 45M rung, {{tail_xl93lr40_5000}} against\n{{tail_l45_5000}} over the last 5,000 steps, and reads worse on four of the\nfive frame groups. A larger model fitting its own training data worse at\nmatched steps is the signature of undertraining. What that pair measures is\nthat at a fixed token budget doubling the parameters costs, which is what a\nscaling law predicts, and it is a different statement from the one this ladder\nwas built to make.\n\nSo the claim that more parameters do not buy surface generalisation here is\nnot supported by the pair and is not made. `xl93match` is the run that\nseparates the two readings: the same rung at the same rate for\n{{steps_match_xl93}} steps, which is the 45M rung's tokens per parameter\nrather than its step count, and nothing else changed. If it then beats the 45M\nrung on the trained and held-out-question groups, the parameter axis was\nalways fine and the earlier reading was undertraining. If it beats the 45M\nrung everywhere except the held-out sentence mode, that is the dissociation\nworth having, and it is much stronger than what is here now, because the\nlarger model would then be better at everything except the compositional axis.\n\nOne caveat on the arithmetic itself. The run makes {{budget_epochs}} passes\nover a file of {{budget_examples}} examples, so these are not unique tokens\nand the usual tokens-per-parameter guidance, which assumes they are, applies\nloosely. The undertraining reading does not rest on that guidance. It rests on\nthe direct observation that at equal steps the larger rung fit its own\ntraining data less well, and more steps at the same rate is the direct remedy\nfor that.\n\n### Two of the four rungs cannot reach a comparable budget on this card\n\n{{TOKEN_BUDGET}}\n\nAt the 45M rung's tokens per parameter the 167M rung needs\n{{steps_match_xxl167}} steps and the 355M rung {{steps_match_xxxl355}}. On one\nL40S that is about fourteen hours and about sixty-four. The 167M rung is\nqueued at its matched budget and runs last. The 355M rung is not run at all.\nA 355M rung reported as flat after seeing an eighth of the tokens per\nparameter the 45M rung saw would be a statement about its budget that reads as\na statement about the architecture, and that is worse than leaving the top of\nthe ladder empty.\n\nThe ladder therefore does not reach 350M, and the question of where surface\ngeneralisation appears between 45M and 350M is not answered here. What is\nanswered is narrower and is the part the card can pay for.",
